<p>When you receive a bonus of ₹3 lakhs, the question is not whether to save it but where it does the most work. Prepaying a ₹30 lakh home loan at 8.5% in Year 3 with that ₹3 lakhs saves approximately ₹5.4 lakh in total interest and cuts over 20 months off the tenure — a guaranteed, risk-free return equivalent to 8.5% on the prepaid amount. An equity mutual fund might return more over a decade, but prepayment returns are certain while investment returns are not. For borrowers who value certainty, prepayment is often the optimal use of a windfall, particularly early in the loan when the principal-to-interest ratio is most unfavourable.</p>

<p>The regulatory context matters critically here. Under RBI's Master Direction on Interest Rate on Advances, banks and lending institutions <strong>cannot levy any prepayment or foreclosure charges on floating-rate loans to individual borrowers</strong>. This means for a floating-rate home loan, personal loan, or car loan, you can prepay any amount at any time with zero penalty. Fixed-rate loans are exempt from this prohibition — your lender may charge 2–4% on the prepaid amount for fixed-rate facilities, which must be factored into whether prepayment is still worthwhile. Check your loan sanction letter's prepayment clause before transferring funds. Use the <a href="/india/home-loan-emi-calculator/">Home Loan EMI Calculator</a> to confirm your current EMI and outstanding balance, and the <a href="/india/loan-eligibility-calculator/">Loan Eligibility Calculator</a> if you plan to take a fresh loan after prepaying.</p>
